Part 1 - 
---------------------------------------------------------
1. Row Key and Sharding
The proposed row key for this dataset is a composite of 
State#City#VIN. This design balances write distribution 
and query efficiency. The VIN component guarantees 
uniqueness for every record, while the geographic prefix 
groups related vehicles together for efficient range scans. 
Hotspotting occurs when too many writes concentrate on the 
same tablet server — for example, using Model Year alone 
as a row key would send every 2024 registration to a single 
node. The composite key avoids this because writes are naturally 
spread across hundreds of city prefixes across the state, 
and no single prefix dominates traffic volume. For querying by 
City, this row key is efficient. A prefix scan on WA#Seattle# 
retrieves all Seattle vehicles in one contiguous range scan 
without touching other tablets. Querying by Make, however,
is not directly supported by this row key since Make does 
not appear in the key, finding all Tesla vehicles requires 
a full table scan. To handle Make-based queries efficiently, 
a secondary index table with row key Make#City#VIN would be
maintained in parallel.

2. Column Families
Bigtable stores each column family in a separate SSTable file 
on disk, meaning reads only load the families they actually need.
The proposed design uses four column families grouped by access
pattern. The first, vehicle_identity, contains VIN, Make, Model,
Model Year, and Electric Vehicle Type. This family is queried most
frequently and keeping it isolated means a count-by-make query 
loads only this small family rather than pulling in unrelated data. 
The second family, location, contains County, City, State, Postal Code, 
Legislative District, Census Tract, and GPS coordinates. Geographic 
attributes are almost always queried together for regional analysis, 
so isolating them prevents map-visualization workloads from loading 
pricing or utility data unnecessarily. The third family, ev_specs, 
holds Electric Range, Base MSRP, and CAFV Eligibility. These specification
fields are queried primarily for analytical workloads such as computing 
average range by make, and separating them avoids loading spec data during 
simple vehicle lookups. The fourth family, utility, contains Electric
Utility and DOL Vehicle ID, which are administrative fields queried 
rarely and can be stored with lower cache priority.
This design improves distributed read performance because when a query targets
only Make and Model Year, Bigtable loads only the vehicle_identity SSTable 
files for the relevant tablet — the other three families are never read from disk. 
Different families can also be cached independently across nodes, allowing hot
analytical families to stay in memory while cold administrative data is evicted.

3. Horizontal Scaling
A hashed shard key on VIN distributes writes perfectly uniformly across
all nodes because VINs produce random hash outputs with no clustering. 
This eliminates hotspotting even during write spikes such as end-of-year 
vehicle registration surges. Lookups by a single VIN are also efficient
since the hash function routes directly to exactly one shard in O(1) time. 
The major drawback is that range queries become impossible as retrieving all 2022
vehicles requires querying every shard simultaneously (scatter-gather), 
which increases latency and cluster load proportionally to the number of shards. 
Rebalancing is straightforward with consistent hashing since data redistributes 
evenly when nodes are added.
A range-based shard key on Model Year has the opposite tradeoff.
Range scans are highly efficient — a query for all 2020–2022 vehicles reads a 
small contiguous set of tablets. However, this design causes severe hotspotting in practice. 
All newly registered vehicles in the current model year write to the same shard,
creating a bottleneck while older year shards sit idle. Cluster rebalancing also 
becomes uneven since popular year ranges produce large tablets that split 
frequently while sparse years remain small.
For this EV dataset, where the dominant workload is individual vehicle 
lookups and high-throughput registration inserts, the hashed shard key
on VIN is the better choice. It prevents write hotspotting at the cost
of scatter-gather for year-based analytics. If the primary workload 
were temporal batch analysis such as computing adoption trends across model years. 
A range-based key with a salted prefix to mitigate hotspotting would be more appropriate.

Part 4 - 
---------------------------------------------------------
The measured latency difference between w=1 (~107ms avg) 
and w=majority (~104ms avg) was negligible (~3ms) because 
all three Atlas replica nodes are co-located within the 
same cloud region. The dominant cost (~100ms) is the network
round-trip between the GCP VM and the Atlas cluster, which
both write concerns must pay equally. The theoretical 
advantage of w=1 which skips the secondary acknowledgment  
only becomes significant in geo-distributed deployments 
where secondaries are in different regions, potentially 
adding 50–200ms per write. 
CAP Theorem scenario for w=1: A real-time multiplayer 
game recording millions of player movement events per second 
is a strong use case for w=1. Under CAP, this prioritizes
Availability and Partition Tolerance over Consistency as 
losing a few position-update writes during a primary failover
is acceptable because the data is transient, immediately 
superseded by the next update, and no financial or safety
consequence follows from the loss.